  "nameStory": "The Meadow Mushroom earned its name from its favorite hangout spots – open grassy meadows, pastures, and lawns where it pops up like nature's own yard decorations. In Europe, it's called the \"field mushroom\" for the same reason, as it loves those wide-open spaces where grass grows thick and soil stays rich.",
  "description": "The Meadow Mushroom is a classic white-capped fungus that looks remarkably similar to the button mushrooms you see in grocery stores – and for good reason, they're close cousins! This chunky mushroom can grow up to about 5 inches across with a bright white cap that starts dome-shaped and flattens out as it matures. You'll find dark chocolate-brown to nearly black gills underneath that contrast beautifully with the pale cap, making it quite striking when you flip it over to take a look.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Start bright pink when young, then darken to rich chocolate brown or nearly black as spores mature. The gills are free from the stem and packed tightly together like the pages of a book.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Pure white to cream-colored, smooth and dry to the touch, starting dome-shaped but flattening with age. Can reach up to 5 inches across and feels firm and meaty.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "White and sturdy with a simple white ring around the upper portion that you can slide up and down like a loose bracelet. The stem is solid or has a small hollow center.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Dark chocolate brown to nearly black – place the cap gill-side down on white paper overnight and you'll see this distinctive dark print.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Pleasant mushroomy aroma, similar to store-bought mushrooms but earthier and more intense. Some people detect a slight anise or almond-like scent.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bruising",
      "description": "May yellow slightly when cut or bruised, but this reaction is usually faint and slow. The flesh stays mostly white and feels firm like a fresh mushroom should.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Meadow Mushrooms in grassy areas throughout New England – suburban lawns, park fields, pastures, and meadows with rich, well-fertilized soil. They particularly love spots where horses or cattle graze, as the nutrient-rich soil gives them everything they need. You'll often find them growing in small groups or fairy rings in areas that get regular moisture but aren't waterlogged.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Occasionally appears in late spring during warm, wet periods, but this is not their prime time. Look after several days of rain when temperatures stay consistently mild.",
    "summer": "Peak season! Look for fresh flushes after summer thunderstorms, especially in July and August when warm temperatures and regular moisture create perfect growing conditions.",
    "fall": "Still actively fruiting through early fall, particularly after autumn rains. September can be excellent for finding fresh specimens in good condition.",
    "winter": "Rarely found, though in mild New England winters you might spot the occasional late fruiting during warm spells, but don't count on it."
  },
  "funFact": "The Meadow Mushroom is essentially the wild ancestor of the button mushrooms you buy at the grocery store – but here's the kicker: the wild version often tastes significantly better, with a more complex, nutty flavor that puts its commercially grown cousin to shame!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most dangerously confused with young Destroying Angel mushrooms, which are pure white but have white gills (not pink/brown) and a bulbous base. Remember this lifesaver: \"Pink gills good, white gills – don't risk it!\" Also confused with the Yellow Stainer, which bruises bright yellow when cut and smells unpleasant. SAFETY NOTE: Never eat any wild mushroom without expert identification – some look-alikes are deadly poisonous.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being closely related to grocery store button mushrooms (Agaricus bisporus), the Meadow Mushroom represents what mushrooms tasted like before commercial cultivation. Many European field guides still call this the \"true\" field mushroom, as it was likely one of the first wild mushrooms regularly gathered by humans for food.",
  "compareHints": "Among its Agaricus relatives, the Meadow Mushroom is distinguished by its preference for open grasslands rather than woodlands and its relatively mild bruising reaction. The Horse Mushroom is much larger and has a stronger anise smell, while the Yellow Stainer bruises bright yellow and smells phenolic (like paint or ink). The Wood Mushroom prefers forested areas and typically has more pronounced yellowing when bruised.",
